  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Off-market new home investment opportunities usually come from builders, agents and land owners who would rather sell quietly and quickly than list publicly. To find them you need relationships, a clear buying profile, and the ability to close when a deal appears. This guide covers where those deals come from and how to vet them without getting burned.</p>

<h2>Why builders sell new homes off-market</h2>
<p>It helps to understand the seller's side. A builder or developer may skip the MLS when:</p>
<ul>
<li>A buyer's contract fell through late in construction and they want the home sold fast.</li>
<li>A phase is nearly finished and the last few homes are holding up the close-out.</li>
<li>Spec homes are carrying construction loan interest every month they sit.</li>
<li>They want to avoid public price cuts that could affect appraisals on the rest of the community.</li>
</ul>
<p>In each case, the builder values speed and certainty. If you can offer both, you become the buyer they call first. That is often worth more than a lowball offer.</p>

<h2>Where to find off-market new homes</h2>
<h3>1. Builder sales offices</h3>
<p>Walk into model homes and talk to the sales staff. Tell them you are an investor who can close in a set number of days and ask about spec inventory, cancelled contracts and quick-move-in homes. Leave your contact information and follow up every few weeks. Smaller local builders are often more flexible than national ones.</p>

<h3>2. Agents who specialize in new construction</h3>
<p>Some agents work mostly with builders and hear about inventory before it goes public. Ask for pocket listings and pre-market opportunities. Be specific: price range, areas, bed and bath count, and whether you will rent or resell.</p>

<h3>3. Land owners and lot sellers</h3>
<p>If you are open to building yourself, finished lots in established subdivisions are sometimes sold privately. Check county appraisal district records for vacant lots owned by individuals or small LLCs, and reach out with a short, respectful letter. Our guide on <a href="/choosing-the-right-location-for-your-texas-dream-home/">choosing the right location</a> covers what makes a lot worth buying.</p>

<h3>4. Wholesalers and investor networks</h3>
<p>Local real estate investor meetups, online groups and wholesalers can surface deals, including newer homes owned by people who need to sell. Vet wholesalers carefully and verify that they actually have the property under contract.</p>

<h3>5. Homes with insurance or storm history</h3>
<p>After hail, wind or water events, some owners of newer homes decide to sell rather than manage repairs. These can be good opportunities if you understand the claim history. Ask whether a claim was filed and how it was settled. A licensed public adjuster firm such as <a href="https://txpublicadjusting.com/">TX Public Adjusting</a> can explain how storm claims typically work in Texas so you know what questions to ask the seller.</p>

<h3>6. Direct outreach and driving neighborhoods</h3>
<p>Drive new subdivisions and note homes that look finished but unoccupied, or homes with long-running "for sale by owner" signs. Public records will show the owner. Direct mail and polite door knocking still work, especially when you are clear about what you can offer.</p>

<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Keep a one-page "buyer profile" ready to email: your target areas, price range, how fast you close, and a pre-approval letter or proof of funds. Builders and agents remember the investor who made their job easy.</p></div>

<h2>How to vet an off-market new home</h2>
<p>A quiet sale has no other buyers to keep the price honest, so the due diligence is on you.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Run your own comps.</strong> Pull recent sales of similar homes nearby and compare price per square foot.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate rent conservatively.</strong> Check active rental listings for similar homes and use the middle of the range.</li>
<li><strong>Review the builder warranty.</strong> Know what is covered, for how long, and whether it transfers.</li>
<li><strong>Check HOA rules.</strong> Some communities restrict leasing or short-term rentals.</li>
<li><strong>Verify taxes.</strong> New homes are often assessed only on the land in year one. Estimate taxes on the full finished value.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect it.</strong> Hire an independent inspector even on brand-new homes. Our <a href="/new-construction-inspection-checklist/">new construction inspection checklist</a> shows what to look for.</li>
</ol>
<p>When you walk the property yourself, a few tools help you spot problems the seller may not mention.</p>
<!--products:laser_measure,moisture_meter,thermal_camera|Tools for walking an off-market deal-->
<p>A [[amz:laser_measure|laser measure]] lets you check that the square footage matches what you were told, and a [[amz:thermal_camera|thermal camera]] can reveal missing insulation or hidden moisture behind drywall.</p>

<h2>Negotiating with builders</h2>
<p>Builders often prefer giving incentives over cutting the sticker price, because a lower recorded price affects appraisals in the community. Ask about:</p>
<ul>
<li>Closing cost help or rate buydowns through their preferred lender</li>
<li>Upgrades included at no charge, such as blinds, appliances or landscaping</li>
<li>Extended warranty coverage</li>
<li>Bulk pricing if you buy more than one home</li>
</ul>
<p>Get every promise in writing in the contract or an addendum.</p>

<h2>Risks to keep in mind</h2>
<p>Off-market does not mean risk-free. Watch for overpriced deals presented as "exclusive," unclear title, unfinished items the builder never completes, and rental markets saturated by investors who bought in the same subdivision. No strategy guarantees a return, and this article is general education rather than financial or legal advice. Have a real estate attorney or experienced agent review contracts you are unsure about.</p>
<p>A good book on deal analysis helps you stay disciplined. [[amz:books_millionaire_re|The Millionaire Real Estate Investor]] has a useful section on building lead sources and evaluating deals.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
